# KEYRING_MODE   Graphical session Secret Service policy: auto (default), login,
#                session, or off. Applies to the common X11 startup path and
#                GUI Chrome, whether launched by LightDM or agetty/xinit.
# auto           Password-authenticated PAM logins require an unlocked login
#                collection. Passwordless sessions use the unlocked in-memory
#                session collection only when no persistent default/login
#                collection exists. Existing persistent collections are reused
#                only when already unlocked; never overwritten or decrypted
#                with an empty or hard-coded password.
# login          Require the existing PAM-unlocked persistent collection.
# session        Use transient RAM-only storage, but refuse to replace any
#                existing persistent default/login collection.
# off            Do not initialize keyring or force Chrome's password store.
#                An explicit administrator opt-out, not an automatic fallback.
#
# A session collection is lost when its daemon/session ends. Chrome profiles
# using transient keys must also be disposable: stored passwords and encrypted
# cookies cannot be promised to survive reboot. Persistent profiles need login
# mode and a provisioned encrypted keyring/unlock mechanism. Automatic login
# is NOT a way to recover a password for an existing encrypted keyring.
#
# TS_KEYRING_AUTH is a non-secret PAM-generated mode hint, not a credential.
# Never put keyring passwords in thinstation.conf, process arguments or logs.
# Console-only server images that omit gnome-core do not gain keyring PAM or
# X11 startup hooks. The static/default autologin setting is not changed here.
# Multiple concurrent sessions sharing one user bus also share its keyring;
# configure one compatible policy for that user.
